body:
@YuriSizov I think you've understood the technical details that I wanted to make sure we were on the same page on. Unfortunately, you have still not understood the key thing I have been repeating:

This is about ensuring behavioural _consistency_ between non-inherited scenes and inherited scenes, which you are consistently reframing as me wanting to "make an exception". In my first and second screenshots, the behaviour is entirely inconsistent, because of the conveniently chosen implementation for inherited scenes. Conceptually, a "fancy bowl" should not be duplicating things any more than a plain "bowl".

As for your use case, yes I agree, I have always wanted Godot to have a way to edit resources, and not just node properties, in a non-permanent way. Kind of like a non-exported property. Local-to-session changes, if you will. However, I also understand that would be a _huge_ overhaul. Hence why the smaller-scope fix. I completely disagree with the framing of this as a "core engine change" in a negative sense. Core features with special editor considerations exist in abundance, and that's unavoidable. Property usage flags are one example.

> If, as you propose, when opening an inherited scene resources belonging to the base scene are not duplicated, these two cases are not achievable. The resources will be effectively shared between the base scene and the inherited scene, within the editor. There will be only one initialization, for one set of data, and tool scripts in both scenes will be operating on the same data. I would make a claim that this equally breaks user expectations. At least this user and his expectations 🙃

@YuriSizov Your described use case currently _only_ works on inherited scenes, and _not_ on non-inherited scenes, precisely because of this inconsistency bug. Any tool script that modifies a local-to-scene material on an open, non-inherited scene would still end up modifying the underlying resource, affecting every other open scene which happens to use that resource.

If you're saying that the editor should just behave like runtime and local-to-scene resources should always duplicate when opening up a scene so you don't end up accidentally editing what's potentially shared with other scenes, then non-inherited scenes shouldn't be special either.

@YuriSizov 

I am sorry if I have made your views seem unwelcome, that's not the case at all. I tagged you first, after all, you did not inject yourself. I am not invested in my proposed fix being the final fix, but it was frustrating to feel misunderstood, and to hear you assert that I was approaching the problem from an angle of exceptionalism. The lack of acknowledgement of certain points I made caused me to think some info was amiss. And in my attempts to find and bridge potential gaps, I ended up over-explaining things you already understood.

We are coming at this from very different perspectives with different priorities. You value systemic consistency (inherited scene is an instance, therefore), whereas I am looking for UX consistency (opening inherited scene is opening a scene, therefore). I may not agree fully with you, but they are both valid perspectives, so thank you for sharing.
